        'A different contractor',
        'The visit does not happen',
        'Cover from the same team, already briefed on your site',
      ],
    },
    {
      label: 'How the scope is written',
      values: [
        'A standard schedule applied to your site',
        'Usually verbal',
        'Grouped by infection risk, the way your own cleaning policy is grouped',
      ],
    },
    {
      label: 'Personal care',
      values: [
        'Policy varies',
        'Sometimes helps informally',
        'Never. Our team finds a staff member instead',
      ],
    },
    {
      label: 'Clinical waste and terminal cleans',
      values: [
        'Sometimes offered',
        'Sometimes offered',
        'Not undertaken. Those need clinically trained staff',
      ],
    },
    {
      label: 'Periodic work',
      values: [
        'Quoted separately when it looks bad',
        'Usually not scheduled',
        'On the schedule with a named month against each item',
      ],
    },
    {
      label: 'Commitment',
      values: ['Often a fixed term', 'None', 'No lock-in, no minimum term, no exit fee'],
    },
  ],
};

// §10 Branded FAQs, in the CUSTOMER'S voice — here that is an operator, a
// village manager or a board member.
const brandedFaqItems = [
  {
    question: 'Do you take single units, or only whole sites?',
    answer:
      // ⚠️ Real bracket, and it decides who this page is for.
      'We quote whole sites and we also do independent living unit turnovers between residents, which is closer to an end of lease clean than to a regular visit. [Whether TLB takes individual occupied units inside a village, and whether that is billed to the village or the resident. Confirm.] If a resident wants their own unit cleaned privately, we are happy to arrange that separately from your contract.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Are your cleaners screened?',
    answer:
      // ⚠️ This is the page's gate, not a to-do. See the file header.
      'Our cleaners are employed by TLB rather than subcontracted, which is what makes screening possible to evidence at all: there is no third party in between holding the paperwork. [TLB’s screening: what checks are held for every cleaner, how often they are renewed, and what evidence can be produced on request. Confirm before this page ships.] Ask us for it in writing at the walkthrough and hold us to producing it.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you work around care routines and meal times?',
    answer:
      // Brief §25. BFD §9.2 bans em dashes outright. Recast with commas, no
      // item lost. Protected otherwise: listing meals, medication rounds,
      // physio, visiting hours and rest periods as the things the schedule is
      // built backwards from is the operational detail that tells a facility
      // manager TLB has done this before.
      'Yes, and the schedule is built backwards from them. We take your daily rhythm at the walkthrough, meaning meals, medication rounds, physio, visiting hours and when residents nap, and place each area in the gaps. Dining rooms get done between sittings. Corridors get done in halves. Nothing noisy happens before eight or during a rest period.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What happens during an outbreak?',
    answer:
      'We follow your infection control procedures, not our own, because you are the ones accountable for them. In practice that usually means increased high-touch cleaning, separate equipment for affected areas and our team following the same PPE and movement rules as your staff. What we do not do is terminal cleans or decontamination, which need clinically trained staff. We will say so rather than take the work.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Will it be the same cleaners each time?',
    answer:
      'Yes, and on this page that answer matters more than on any other. You get the same small team, they meet your staff before they start, and they are briefed on your site rather than handed an address. When someone takes leave, their cover comes from the same team and has already walked the site. Residents should recognise the person cleaning their room.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What happens if I get in touch?',
    answer:
      'We walk the site with you, including the areas that never make it onto a quote: the sluice room, the laundry, the back of the kitchen. Then you get a written scope grouped the way your own cleaning policy is grouped, a price per visit, and a schedule for the periodic work. No obligation.',
  },
];

// §11 Non-branded FAQs, the long-tail block.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED, and this block is the most legally
// sensitive on the whole cluster. Every answer below is deliberately general
// and points the reader at a primary source rather than asserting a standard:
//   - The Aged Care Quality Standards do require a safe, clean and
//     comfortable service environment. Correct in substance, and named
//     without quoting a standard number, since the framework has been
//     revised.
//   - Cleaning is not clinical care and cleaners are not care workers.
//     Correct and important — it is the distinction the last answer makes.
//   - Nothing here should be read as compliance advice, and the answers say
//     so where it matters.
const nonBrandedFaqItems = [
  {
    question: 'How much does aged care cleaning cost?',
    answer:
      // ⚠️ BRACKETED ON PURPOSE, same as every sibling page.
      'It is priced per visit against a written scope. The number moves with how many resident rooms and bathrooms there are, how much communal and corridor floor, whether wet areas and laundries are included, and how many visits a day the dining rooms need. A village of independent living units and a residential care home are different jobs at different prices. [TLB’s pricing basis. Confirm.]',
  },
  {
    question: 'What are the cleaning requirements for aged care homes in Australia?',
    answer:
      'Approved providers are required to give residents a service environment that is safe, clean, well maintained and comfortable, and that obligation sits within the Aged Care Quality Standards. The Standards set the outcome rather than a task list, so each provider translates it into its own cleaning policy, schedule and records. Your own policy is the document a cleaning contractor should be quoting against. This is general information, not compliance advice.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How often should aged care facilities be cleaned?',
    answer:
      'High-touch surfaces and shared bathrooms are daily at minimum, and more often in a home with higher care needs. Dining rooms need cleaning between sittings rather than once a day. Resident rooms are usually daily for floors and bins with a fuller clean weekly. Corridors, handrails and communal lounges are daily. Carpet extraction, upholstery and high dusting are periodic, on a set month.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is high-touch cleaning?',
    answer:
      'It is the targeted cleaning and disinfecting of the surfaces hands actually land on: handrails, door handles and push plates, light switches, lift buttons, bed and chair rails, tap handles and shared appliance handles. It matters because infection moves by contact far more than by floor, and yet floors are what a thin scope spends its time on because floors are what people see.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Do aged care cleaners need police checks?',
    answer:
      'Providers are accountable for screening the people who work with residents, and that extends to contractors as well as their own staff. In practice that means a cleaning company working on an aged care site should be able to evidence current screening for every individual who attends, not just assert it at a company level. Ask for it per person and ask when each one was last renewed.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can a cleaner help a resident with personal care?',
    answer:
      'No, and a cleaning company that lets it happen informally is creating a serious problem for the provider. Helping someone up, into a shower or in and out of bed is personal care, it requires training and it sits under the provider’s own duty of care. Our team is briefed to help by finding a staff member, and nothing else. It is not unkindness. It is the line that keeps residents safe.',
  },
];

// §12 Closing CTA.
const closingCta = {
  kicker: 'Aged care and retirement living',
  heading: 'Start with a walk of the site.',
  lead: 'Including the sluice room, the laundry and the back of the kitchen. Then a written scope grouped the way your cleaning policy is, and a price per visit.',
  cta: walkthroughCta,
};

const business = commercialBusiness(pageMeta.description);
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="left"
      kicker={hero.kicker}
      headingLines={hero.headingLines}
      lead={hero.lead}
      cta={hero.cta}
      image={hero.image}
    />

    <Backdrop image={definitionBgImg} tint="muted" opacity={75}>
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={trustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock theme="muted" align="left" body={definitionBody} />
    </Backdrop>

    {/* §3 — deliberately above every piece of commercial copy on the page.
        See the file header: the mega-menu label "and seniors" sends family
        members here and the kindest thing this page can do is send them on. */}
    <Callout
      tone="note"
      heading={wrongPageCallout.heading}
      body={wrongPageCallout.body}
      cta={wrongPageCallout.cta}
    />

    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="icon-grid"
      heading="What goes on the scope"
      lead="Grouped by infection risk, the way your own cleaning policy is grouped, rather than room by room."
      items={scopeItems}
      cta={walkthroughCta}
    />

    <StoryMosaic theme="dark" heading="An office is empty. A village never is." blocks={residentsBlocks} />

    <TextBlock
      theme="light"
      align="left"
      heading="The two questions worth more than the quote"
      body={worthKnowingBody}
    />

    <TagCloud
      heading="Other premises we clean"
      subheading="Commercial cleaning for every kind of site across the Northern Rivers and the Tweed."
      tags={relatedTags}
      note={[]}
    />

    <ComparisonTable
      heading={comparisonTable.heading}
      cornerLabel={comparisonTable.cornerLabel}
      lead={comparisonTable.lead}
      columns={comparisonTable.columns}
      rows={comparisonTable.rows}
    />

    <TestimonialCarousel heading="What operators and managers say" testimonials={testimonials} uniform />

    <TagCloud
      heading="Where we clean"
      subheading={footprintSubheading}
      groups={footprintLocationGroups}
      note={["If a town isn't listed, ask us anyway."]}
    />

    {/* §1.3 The Line as a section heading, with §1.4 as its standfirst.
        The copy, and the reason this page gets `fast` rather than the other
        variant, both live in data/brand-lines.ts. Change it there, not here.

        `muted`, not `dark`: TagCloud above paints --color-brand and Faq below
        paints --color-surface, so Cream between them keeps the page
        alternating instead of stacking two dark bands.

        No CTA prop. §9.5 wants one primary CTA per page, repeated, and the
        closing CallToAction is directly under the FAQs. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading={theLineHeading.fast}
      body={[managedForYou.recurring, ...theLineBody.commercial]}
    />

    {/* Brief §22 and §23. The page carries two FAQ headings and the generic
        one made the split look like a build error. The second heading also
        lands the mapped phrase contiguously, which is the 10-point H2
        component the page previously half scored, and drops Title Case,
        which BFD §9.2 bans. */}
    <Faq heading="Working with us on your site" items={brandedFaqItems} />
    <Faq heading="Aged care cleaning services, common questions" items={nonBrandedFaqItems} />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={closingCta.kicker}
      heading={closingCta.heading}
      lead={closingCta.lead}
      cta={closingCta.cta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
